{
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Cloudflare Worker — {{app_id}}-api. Wrangler v4 / jsonc.
  //
  // THIS FILE ONLY EXISTS BECAUSE THE APP WAS STAMPED WITH needs_backend=true.
  // The DEFAULT stamp is client-only: no Worker, no D1 and no R2 of its own —
  // it talks to the ONE shared `platform` Worker for config, analytics,
  // entitlements and account deletion.
  //
  // ── THE CLONE CONTRACT (what may and may not be per-app) ──────────────────
  //   PER-APP   : APP_DB (this D1 only), ALLOWED_ORIGINS, APP_ID
  //   SHARED    : PLATFORM_DB (one entitlements table for the whole portfolio),
  //               JWKS_CACHE, SESSION_REVOKED, SUPABASE_URL (one identity
  //               project, so one revocation list),
  //               R2 (ONE portfolio bucket keyed by an `<app_id>/` prefix — a
  //               per-app bucket is never stamped)
  //   NEVER HERE: MoR/payment webhooks. They terminate in `platform`, which
  //               already owns `platform_db.entitlements` keyed by
  //               (user_id, app_id, entitlement). N webhook endpoints would
  //               mean N provider notification destinations and N copies of one
  //               signing secret, for zero benefit.
  //
  // ⚠️ D1 FREE TIER = 10 DATABASES PER ACCOUNT, 5 GB TOTAL (verified against
  //    developers.cloudflare.com/d1/platform/limits, 2026-07-25). platform_db is
  //    one of them, so per-app databases are affordable for at most NINE apps
  //    and the tenth stamp cannot create one at all. That is why a backend is
  //    opt-in: the ceiling gets spent on apps that genuinely store user rows.
  //
  // ⏱ CORRECTED 2026-09-08 — THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE IS LEFT AS WRITTEN AND ITS
  // ARITHMETIC IS DEAD. tooling/ceilings.json:61 records `"cloudflare":
  // "workers-paid"` since 2026-09-03, where the ceiling is 50,000 databases and
  // 10 GB each, so "at most NINE apps" describes a constraint that expired. The
  // live tree was corrected that day and THIS TEMPLATE WAS NOT — the same
  // fixed-the-instance-not-the-class leftover that
  // tooling/ci/assert-clone-contract.mjs:13-40 carries the full correction for,
  // and which services/platform/wrangler.jsonc:10-15 records for the cron cap.
  //
  // 🔴 THE DEFAULT IS STILL CLIENT-ONLY, FOR THESE REASONS AND NOT THE CEILING:
  //   · BLAST RADIUS. One bad migration in a shared database is a portfolio
  //     outage. [ADR 020] rejected the app_id-discriminator design on this.
  //   · MIGRATION COST. `wrangler d1 migrations apply` takes ONE database. N
  //     databases is a loop with no cross-database transactionality.
  //   · YAGNI. An app that does not store user rows server-side should not own a
  //     database, a migration set and an erasure route it never needed.
  //
  // ── AFTER STAMPING — ONE COMMAND, AND NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS HAND-EDITED ──
  //   node tooling/scripts/provision-backend.mjs {{app_id}}
  // It creates the D1 with `--location apac`, WRITES the returned id into
  // APP_DB.database_id below, and applies the starter migration. The
  // all-zeros placeholder is correct until it runs, and `assert-d1-bindings
  // .mjs` fails any config under services/ that still carries one.
  //
  // 🔴 THIS BLOCK USED TO DIRECT THE READER TO TRANSCRIBE THE NEW UUID INTO THE
  // APP_DB KEY BELOW, BY HAND, and went on saying so for a week after
  // `provision-backend.mjs` had removed the need. [3]S-12's requirement is
  // literally "no hand-editing of placeholders", so the template every backend
  // app is stamped from was directing its owner to perform the exact step the
  // requirement exists to delete — a manual copy of a uuid into one key of a
  // JSONC file, which is the shape of edit that goes wrong silently and deploys
  // a Worker reading the wrong database. No guard reached this file:
  // assert-input-contract.mjs scanned three brick files and this was not one,
  // so the instruction was invisible to CI. It is scanned now — see section 4
  // of that guard, which also explains why this paragraph is worded around the
  // retired phrasing rather than quoting it.
  //
  //   `--location apac` is load-bearing, not cosmetic: location_hint is
  //   CREATE-TIME ONLY and can never be changed afterwards, and D1 places the
  //   primary near WHOEVER ISSUED THE CREATE CALL — so provisioning from a US
  //   runner would silently land an India-served database in North America.
  //   tooling/scripts/provision-backend.mjs:16-29 — the script this block tells
  //   you to run — carries the placement half with the date it was checked
  //   against the vendor (2026-07-29) and the full hint set, including why a D1
  //   JURISDICTION is the wrong tool here.
  //
  //   ⏱ THE CITATION HERE WAS `company/requirements/architecture.md §18` UNTIL
  //   2026-09-08 AND THAT DIRECTORY IS GONE (deleted 2026-08-15). It was the
  //   last live reference to it anywhere in this repository, on a line that
  //   justifies a flag which cannot be corrected after the fact — so a reader
  //   checking the claim found nothing and had to take it on trust.
  // Secrets are NOT set here (never committed): set with `wrangler secret put`.
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "name": "{{app_id}}-api",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-01",
  "observability": { "enabled": true },
  "vars": {
    "APP_ID": "{{app_id}}",
    "SUPABASE_URL": "https://auth-api.nikatru.com",
    "API_VERSION": "v1",
    // CLOCK 2026-09-12 DOT THE APEX, NOT A PER-APP SUBDOMAIN. This stamped
    // `https://{{app_id}}.nikatru.com`, which [ADR 075] retired as an app address
    // and [ADR 080] section 4 finished off by deleting the wildcard that made such
    // names resolve at all. So a freshly stamped Worker allowed exactly one origin
    // THAT CANNOT EXIST, and refused the one the app is actually served from:
    // every API call from its own web build would have been blocked by the browser,
    // with no server-side error to find. Since [ADR 075] every app is published at
    // `nikatru.com/<id>`, so the browser origin is the APEX and it is the same for
    // every app.
    //
    // WARN ADD THE PAGES ALIAS TOO IF THE APP IS OPENED DIRECTLY at its
    // `*.pages.dev` production URL. It is NOT derivable - Cloudflare appends a
    // suffix when the project name is taken - so it is a printed checklist step
    // rather than a guess stamped here. `cors.ts` matches exact origins only.
    "ALLOWED_ORIGINS": "https://nikatru.com"
  },
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      // PER-APP. The only resource this Worker owns outright.
      "binding": "APP_DB",
      "database_name": "{{app_id}}_db",
      "database_id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
      "migrations_dir": "migrations"
    },
    {
      // SHARED across the portfolio. `platform` is the sole migrations applier,
      // so there is deliberately NO migrations_dir here.
      "binding": "PLATFORM_DB",
      "database_name": "platform_db",
      "database_id": "9d1c5c63-97fe-4f82-bc7d-f3fd22e9b351"
    }
  ],
  "kv_namespaces": [
    { "binding": "JWKS_CACHE", "id": "bcfcb1618d1a4bfd8d02f87d3d21010d" },
    // The revocation list (`rev:<sub>`), READ ONLY here by
    // src/middleware/auth.ts; services/platform writes it. Shared.
    { "binding": "SESSION_REVOKED", "id": "aa46ad5002874231931cc5dc5b6e2904" }
  ],
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // [pipeline B-15] THE STAMPED BACKEND BINDS ITS OWN HOST — NO MANUAL STEP.
  //
  // 🔴 THIS BLOCK WAS MISSING UNTIL 2026-08-03, AND WITHOUT IT A stamped backend
  // deployed to a `*.workers.dev` name and `{{app_id}}-api.nikatru.com` bound to
  // NOTHING. `services/platform/wrangler.jsonc` has always declared its two
  // hosts this way; the template every future app is stamped from declared none,
  // so "answers on its declared API host" was a manual dashboard step nobody
  // wrote down — the exact class of per-app toil the factory exists to remove.
  //
  // `custom_domain: true` auto-provisions the DNS record AND the edge
  // certificate, which is why `platform.nikatru.com` resolves without anyone
  // creating a record. nikatru.com is a zone in this account.
  //
  // ✅ NO WILDCARD IS INVOLVED ANY MORE, AND THAT MAKES THIS LINE LOAD-BEARING.
  // A proxied `*.nikatru.com` existed from 2026-07-27 until [ADR 080] §4 deleted
  // it on 2026-09-12. While it existed the open question was whether a Custom
  // Domain could be created under a wildcarded zone at all — Cloudflare documents
  // that Custom Domains do not support wildcard records and cannot be created
  // over an existing CNAME — and it was answered by reading the live account on
  // 2026-08-03 (GET /accounts/:id/workers/domains returned three Custom Domains
  // under that zone; the zone's Routes list was EMPTY). With the wildcard gone
  // the question no longer arises, and this route is now the ONLY thing that
  // makes the host resolve: before the first deploy the name is NXDOMAIN, not a
  // 522.
  //
  // ⚠️ The WEB host `{{app_id}}.nikatru.com` is stage 9/10's and stays out.
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "routes": [
    { "pattern": "{{app_id}}-api.nikatru.com", "custom_domain": true }
  ]
  // NO "r2_buckets" HERE ON PURPOSE. Object storage is ONE portfolio-wide
  // bucket bound in `services/platform`, addressed with an `<app_id>/` key
  // prefix. Stamping `{{app_id}}-exports` per app multiplied buckets, lifecycle
  // rules and CORS policies by the number of apps in order to store the same
  // kind of blob under a different name.
}
